  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Heavy downpours exit this morning.\n\nPotential still there for heavy downpours to cause localized\nflooding in NW NC into southside VA as pwats are running 1.7 to\n1.9 inches. Fortunately, convection has diminished so rainfall\nrates are mainly running 1 inch or less. However, there remains\nupstream moderate rain, and portions of NW NC had heavier rain\nearlier.\n\nOnce shortwave associated with Arthur passes east this morning,\nshould see a decreasing rain trend. We dry out today with\nsunshine returning west to east, with a northwest breeze.\nTemperatures will be closer to normal with mid 80s east to 70s\nwest. Humidity levels should drop off by the afternoon as well. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Dry weekend but more storm chances early next \nweek.\n\nHigh pressure follows the front Saturday into Sunday. Models\nagree on a broad trough over the Great Lakes into the mid-MS\nValley heading into Monday swinging through the mountains early\nnext week. This feature with the front and good moisture\nadvection should provide another opportunity for showers and\nstorms, but like previous systems, fronts that come from the\nnorthwest favor the mountains (esp western slopes) for rainfall\nand to a much lesser extent east of the mountains.",
